rsity of the perceptible world. In sum, Lucretius stands as a key figure who fused poetic expression with a systematic atomist philosophy, linking Greek atomism to Roman Epicurean thought and popularizing the view that natural phenomena arise from the interactions of indivisible atoms.", "WORRINGER:WORRINGER, also rendered as Worringer, is the theorist and author referenced in the text. He is noted for two related positions in art history: first, that the Gothic (nomadic) abstract line possesses the power of expression rather than being merely a visual form; and second, for contrasting the abstract with the organic in art history. Together, these points situate WORRINGER/Worringer as a key figure who emphasizes the expressive potential of abstract line and the fundamental dichotomy between abstract and organic modes within Gothic and broader art-historical discourse.", "ASSYRIA:ASSYRIA is a geo region mentioned in the text as the origin of the 'Assyrian line' in the discussion of imperial line types.", "GREECE:GREECE is a geographic region historically described as the cradle of ancient philosophy and is referenced as the source of the “Greek” line in discussions of imperial lines. In certain interpretive frameworks, it is linked to an organic Greek line that subordinates volume and spatiality and, within this discourse, replaces the Egyptian geometrical line, signaling a shift from rigid geometric representation to a more living, flowing notion of form. The descriptions also emphasize Greece’s role in relation to the ancients and the concept of the eternal return, underscoring its significance as a philosophical locus tied to timeless questions of time, recurrence, and meaning. In sum, GREECE is portrayed not only as a historic geographic region but also as a symbolic wellspring for the Greek line and for ancient philosophical traditions that contemplate the nature of space, form, and eternity within broader intellectual and imperial contexts.", "CHINA:CHINA is a geo region mentioned as the source of the 'Chinese line' in the comparison of imperial lines.)<|COMPLETE|>", "BECKETT:BECKETT is presented in the provided descriptions as a person referenced in the text, notably in the phrase “la Beckett.” In that specific instance, the text does not supply explicit attributes or activities about the referenced individual. A separate description identifies BECKETT as a writer, described as the author of works whose characters stake out a territory within the discourse on strata and assemblages. \n\nTaken together, BECKETT is portrayed as a real person who is a writer, with literary works whose characters participate in and contribute to a complex theoretical discourse concerned with strata and assemblages. The descriptions imply that Beckett’s writings engage with these notions, positioning his characters within the broader discourse. \n\nThe data also notes that there are no additional entities matching the specified types found in the provided text beyond BECKETT.", "FRANZ KAKFA:Franz Kafka, a writer, cited in the text as an example of assemblages in the discussion of deterritorialization.)<|COMPLETE|>(\"entity\"", "GALILEO:Galileo Galilei, Italian polymath referenced as the namesake of the Galileo abstract machine", "BACH:Johann Sebastian Bach, German composer referenced as the namesake of the Bach abstract machine", "BEETHOVEN:Ludwig van Beethoven, German composer referenced as the namesake of the Beethoven abstract machine", "INTUITION AS METHOD:A section/concept in Bergson's philosophy describing the method of knowing by intuition to access the conditions of real experience beyond empirical perception; it discusses extending beyond experience and the interpenetration of perception and memory.", "BERGSONISM:The Bergsonian philosophical framework or movement presented in the text, encompassing Bergson's ideas about perception, memory, and duration, and organizing the discussion around the two lines of experience and the role of intuition.", "HAMELIN:Hamelin is a writer referenced by Bergson in the text; he is associated with an Essai sur les éléments principaux de la représentation (dated from 1907), which Bergson says he has in mind as part of his discussion on philosophy and representation.", "KANT:Immanuel Kant, a German philosopher of the 18th century and a central figure in modern philosophy, is presented in the text as the architect of a critical project that overturns the traditional Image of Thought. Through the critique of reason, Kant aims to transform recognition and judgment, replacing naive illusions with a disciplined account of how experience is possible. His Kantian critique is described as challenging Descartes’ Cogito and placing time itself as the form in which the undetermined becomes determinable, thereby grounding the conditions for any possible knowledge.\n\nThe passages treat Kant as addressing the nature of the self and consciousness. The “I am” is discussed as a perception or feeling of existence that persists independently of particular determinations, and the unity of the ego is tied to the interplay of faculties. Kant’s framework distinguishes passive synthesis (receptivity to given data) from active synthesis (the mind’s organizing light), with the ego seeking reality through a test of reality. This leads to a longstanding Kantian division of the aesthetic into an objective element (space) and a subjective element (pleasure and pain), and to broader claims about how the ego unifies itself, balancing the reality principle and the pleasures.\n\nThe text situates Kant with Plato in tracing the operation of transcendental memory within reminiscence, memory, and imagination, all analyzed within the notion of transcendental empiricism. It notes Kant’s radical substitution in negation and his contributions to cosmology and representation, including his handling of the antinomies—conflicts arising from reason’s attempt to comprehend totality. Kant’s ideas on schemata, space, and time illuminate how concepts relate to perception and to transcendental possibilities, showing how intuitions are structured by a priori forms.\n\nA central theme is Kant’s duality of concepts and intuitions, and his thinking about space and time as forms that relate to extensity (space) and intensity (time). He argues that all intuitions are extensive quantities and that space and time condition how we experience objects. Ideas, for Kant, are inherently problematic: problems arise from Ideas, yet the critical operation of reason clarifies which ideas are regulative (guiding understanding toward unity) rather than constitutive, providing objective value and an object-like character without guaranteeing direct possession of the thing-in-itself.\n\nThe discourse emphasizes Kant’s critique of consciousness and reason, the unity and harmony of the faculties (the harmony of the Self), and the elimination or redirection of certain faculties within a systematic account. In this synthesis, Kant’s thought—often set against Descartes’ rationalism—offers a comprehensive account of how perception, cognition, memory, and imagination cohere within a framework of space, time, and the structures of the mind.", "ACHILLES:ACHILLES is a mythological Greek hero who appears in Bergson's philosophical discussion as part of a thought experiment known as Achilles' race, used to illustrate different fluxes of time and multiplicity within duration (durée). In the text, the run of Achilles is referenced as a sequence that can be described as divisible into steps, yet each step involves qualitative change rather than mere quantitative addition. This portrayal demonstrates how time can be experienced as a succession of distinct, qualitatively different phases rather than as a uniform, measurable continuum. Through the figure of Achilles, the discussion highlights the tension between division and transformation within duration, capturing Bergson’s idea that time comprises both subdivisible progress and ongoing qualitative evolution.", "TIME AND FREE WILL:Philosophical work referenced in the text; discusses duration as real succession and memory as virtual coexistence; associated with Bergson's ideas"], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_25d30a397497eac55d0452d7dccf40ef451b242ed3db08a5598cfc5ccc357dc0_v2"}